DEFSTRUCT: accessor / constructor / predicate / copier names are interned in
*PACKAGE* instead of the home package of the structure NAME, and BOA constructor
arglists are ignored.

STATUS: open.  Found 2026-06-28 while implementing DRIBBLE (issue dribble): a
package-qualified struct name produced accessors that could not be referenced
from the intended package, so dribble-state was switched to a plain vector.

== Symptom ==
With *PACKAGE* = COMMON-LISP (as during the prelude load):

    (defstruct (bocl::%foo (:constructor bocl::%make-foo)) bar)
    (bocl::%foo-bar (bocl::%make-foo))   ; => Undefined function: %FOO-BAR
                                         ;    (BOCL::%FOO-BAR is unbound; the
                                         ;     accessor is CL::%FOO-BAR instead)

    ;; BOA constructor arglist ignored — constructor is keyword-based:
    (defstruct (baz (:constructor make-baz (x))) x)
    (make-baz 1)        ; => error: "keyword-argument key is not a symbol: 1"
    (make-baz :x 1)     ; works — the (x) arglist was dropped

== Cause ==
bootstrap/prelude.lisp, the DEFSTRUCT macro (around the conc-name / accessor
generation):
  - conc-name and the default constructor/predicate/copier names are built with
    (intern (%string-append ...)), i.e. interned in the current *PACKAGE*, not in
    (symbol-package name).  Per CLHS 3.4.4 / the DEFSTRUCT description, these
    symbols are created in the package of the structure name.
  - the :constructor option reads only (cadr option) — the constructor NAME — and
    ignores any third element (the BOA lambda list), so every constructor is the
    default keyword constructor.

== Fix sketch ==
  - intern accessor/constructor/predicate/copier symbols in (symbol-package name)
    (e.g. (intern str (symbol-package name))), so a package-qualified struct name
    yields accessors in that same package;
  - honour a BOA constructor arglist: when the :constructor option has a third
    element, generate a constructor with that ordinary lambda list (binding the
    named slots positionally / by &optional / &key as written, others to their
    initforms) instead of the all-keyword constructor.

== Impact ==
Low for existing prelude code (it defines structs with unqualified names while
*PACKAGE* is the intended package, so accessors land correctly, and it uses the
keyword constructor).  Bites any code that uses a package-qualified struct name or
a BOA constructor.

== RESOLVED (v1.5.10) ==
DEFSTRUCT macro (bootstrap/prelude.lisp):
- The accessor / constructor / predicate / copier symbols are now interned in
  (SYMBOL-PACKAGE name) — the struct's home package (via a new STRUCT-PKG binding,
  falling back to *PACKAGE* if NAME is uninterned) — instead of the current
  *PACKAGE*.  A package-qualified struct name now yields accessors in that same
  package (verified: (bocl::%tst-foo-bar ...) is fbound in BOCL and works).
- The :constructor option's third element (a BOA ordinary lambda list) is honoured:
  a new %BOA-LAMBDA-VARS extracts the bound variable names, and the constructor is
  generated with that lambda list — slots it names are set from the bound value,
  slots it omits take their initforms — instead of always the all-keyword
  constructor.  (make-baz 1) and (mkpt 3 [&optional (b 0)]) now work; the default
  keyword constructor is unchanged.
New test_structures "home-package names / BOA constructor".  Full unit suite (42) green.
